        <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5">
          <i data-lucide="file-text" class="w-3.5 h-3.5"></i>
          Applies to: all vendors and admin staff
        </span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="max-w-5xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 py-10 sm:py-12 grid lg:grid-cols-[220px_1fr] gap-8">

    <aside class="hidden lg:block">
      <nav class="sticky top-20 text-sm" aria-label="On this page">
        <p class="text-[11px] uppercase tracking-wider font-semibold text-ink-400 mb-3">On this page</p>
        <ul class="space-y-0.5">
          <li><a href="#overview" class="toc-link block border-l-2 border-transparent pl-3 py-1.5 text-ink-500 hover:text-ink-900 transition">Overview</a></li>
          <li><a href="#why" class="toc-link block border-l-2 border-transparent pl-3 py-1.5 text-ink-500 hover:text-ink-900 transition">Why attributes matter</a></li>
          <li><a href="#anatomy" class="toc-link block border-l-2 border-transparent pl-3 py-1.5 text-ink-500 hover:text-ink-900 transition">Anatomy of an attribute</a></li>
          <li><a href="#values" class="toc-link block border-l-2 border-transparent pl-3 py-1.5 text-ink-500 hover:text-ink-900 transition">Attribute values</a></li>
          <li><a href="#integrity" class="toc-link block border-l-2 border-transparent pl-3 py-1.5 text-ink-500 hover:text-ink-900 transition">Data integrity</a></li>
          <li><a href="#lifecycle" class="toc-link block border-l-2 border-transparent pl-3 py-1.5 text-ink-500 hover:text-ink-900 transition">Lifecycle &amp; permissions</a></li>
          <li><a href="#metadata" class="toc-link block border-l-2 border-transparent pl-3 py-1.5 text-ink-500 hover:text-ink-900 transition">Value metadata</a></li>
          <li><a href="#dos" class="toc-link block border-l-2 border-transparent pl-3 py-1.5 text-ink-500 hover:text-ink-900 transition">Do &amp; don't</a></li>
          <li><a href="#faq" class="toc-link block border-l-2 border-transparent pl-3 py-1.5 text-ink-500 hover:text-ink-900 transition">FAQ</a></li>
        </ul>
      </nav>
    </aside>

    <article class="prose-doc min-w-0">

      <section id="overview">
        <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-ink-900 mb-3">Overview</h2>
        <p>
          An <strong>attribute</strong> is a marketplace-level, system-verified property that
          describes a class of products. Examples on COCOCE include
          <code>Color</code>, <code>RAM Type</code>, <code>Storage</code>, <code>Screen Size</code>,
          and <code>Material</code>.
        </p>
        <p>
          Attributes are not products. They are the vocabulary vendors use when listing products.
          A single attribute — say <code>Color</code> — can apply to thousands of listings across
          every store on the marketplace. Its <strong>values</strong> (<em>Red</em>, <em>Black</em>,
          <em>Blue</em>) are what actually attach to a product when it's listed.
        </p>

        <div class="callout callout-info">
          <i data-lucide="info" class="w-5 h-5 callout-icon"></i>
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed">
              <strong>Mental model.</strong> Think of attributes as the columns of a spreadsheet
              that every vendor shares, and values as the cells that get filled in per listing.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="why" class="mt-10">
        <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-ink-900 mb-3">Why attributes matter</h2>
        <p>
          A marketplace without shared attributes is a pile of unstructured text. Buyers can't
          filter, the platform can't compare, and search returns noise. Attributes solve that
          by enforcing a shared, verified vocabulary at three levels:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>For buyers</strong> — Enables reliable filters, side-by-side comparison,
            and accurate search facets (e.g. &ldquo;Android phones with 8GB RAM or more&rdquo;).</li>
          <li><strong>For vendors</strong> — Reduces listing-time errors, gives visibility in
            filtered navigation, and lets products surface where buyers are looking.</li>
          <li><strong>For the platform</strong> — Powers recommendations, category insights,
            fraud detection, and any downstream tooling that needs structured product data.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Every attribute you add to the registry makes the entire marketplace — not just your
          store — more structured and more useful. That's why the registry is shared, and why
          changes to it are governed.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="anatomy" class="mt-10">
        <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-ink-900 mb-3">Anatomy of an attribute</h2>
        <p>Every attribute in the registry has a fixed set of fields:</p>

        <dl class="rounded-xl border border-ink-200 divide-y divide-ink-100 overflow-hidden not-prose">
          <div class="flex items-start justify-between gap-4 px-4 py-3">
            <dt class="text-xs font-semibold text-ink-700 flex-shrink-0 w-32">Title</dt>
            <dd class="text-sm text-ink-700 text-right">
              Human-readable name — <em>Color</em>, <em>RAM Type</em>. Unique across the registry.
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div class="flex items-start justify-between gap-4 px-4 py-3">
            <dt class="text-xs font-semibold text-ink-700 flex-shrink-0 w-32">Description</dt>
            <dd class="text-sm text-ink-700 text-right">
              Explains what the attribute means and when to use it. Optional but strongly encouraged.
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div class="flex items-start justify-between gap-4 px-4 py-3">
            <dt class="text-xs font-semibold text-ink-700 flex-shrink-0 w-32">Status</dt>
            <dd class="text-sm text-ink-700 text-right">
              <code>active</code>, <code>inactive</code>, or <code>requested</code>.
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div class="flex items-start justify-between gap-4 px-4 py-3">
            <dt class="text-xs font-semibold text-ink-700 flex-shrink-0 w-32">Values</dt>
            <dd class="text-sm text-ink-700 text-right">
              The set of allowed options for this attribute. See the next section.
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div class="flex items-start justify-between gap-4 px-4 py-3">
            <dt class="text-xs font-semibold text-ink-700 flex-shrink-0 w-32">Audit trail</dt>
            <dd class="text-sm text-ink-700 text-right">
              Who requested it, when, and who verified or denied it.
            </dd>
          </div>
        </dl>
      </section>

      <section id="values" class="mt-10">
        <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-ink-900 mb-3">Attribute values</h2>
        <p>
          Values are the actual options inside an attribute. <em>Red</em> and <em>Black</em> are
          values of the <code>Color</code> attribute. <em>DDR4</em> and <em>DDR5</em> are values
          of the <code>RAM Type</code> attribute.
        </p>
        <p>
          Values are scoped to their attribute — you cannot have a <em>Red</em> on the
          <code>RAM Type</code> attribute, and you cannot have a <em>DDR4</em> on the
          <code>Color</code> attribute. Each value is unique within its attribute (case-insensitive).
        </p>

        <div class="callout callout-info">
          <i data-lucide="lightbulb" class="w-5 h-5 callout-icon"></i>
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed">
              <strong>Vendors can add values.</strong> If you need <em>Deep Purple</em> for a
              product, you don't need to request it from admin — you can add it directly to the
              <code>Color</code> attribute. It becomes immediately usable and available to every
              other vendor on the marketplace.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="integrity" class="mt-10">
        <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-ink-900 mb-3">Data integrity</h2>
        <p>
          Every attribute and every value you add to the registry is <strong>shared by the entire
            marketplace</strong>. Other vendors will list products under them. Buyers will filter and
          search using them. That's why the registry is governed, and why sloppy additions have
          consequences beyond your store.
        </p>

        <h3 class="mt-6 mb-2 text-base font-semibold text-ink-900">What makes a good attribute</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Universal</strong> — Applies to many products, not just one SKU.
            <em>Color</em> is good. <em>Red iPhone 15 Pro Max</em> is not — that's a product.
          </li>
          <li><strong>Objective</strong> — Has a clear, unambiguous meaning. <em>Screen Size</em> is
            objective; <em>Looks Nice</em> is not.</li>
          <li><strong>Non-redundant</strong> — Doesn't duplicate an existing attribute. Search the
            registry first before requesting a new one.</li>
          <li><strong>Well-named</strong> — Title case, singular, no abbreviations:
            <em>RAM Type</em>, not <em>ram_type</em> or <em>RAMs</em>.
          </li>
        </ul>

        <h3 class="mt-6 mb-2 text-base font-semibold text-ink-900">What makes a good value</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Standard format</strong> — Match existing conventions in the same attribute.
            If siblings use <em>128GB</em>, don't introduce <em>128 GB</em> or <em>128gb</em>.</li>
          <li><strong>Canonical spelling</strong> — <em>Grey</em> and <em>Gray</em> are duplicates.
            Use the spelling already present in the attribute.</li>
          <li><strong>Self-contained</strong> — A value should make sense on its own:
            <em>DDR4</em>, not <em>Type 4</em>.
          </li>
          <li><strong>No ranges or combos</strong> — <em>8–16GB</em> is two values, not one.
            Split them.</li>
        </ul>

        <div class="callout callout-warn">
          <i data-lucide="alert-triangle" class="w-5 h-5 callout-icon"></i>
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed">
              <strong>Duplicates degrade the catalog.</strong> Adding <em>Blue</em> when
              <em>Blue</em> already exists (even with different capitalization or spacing) creates
              two filter entries that show identical results, and splits product data across
              both. The platform rejects exact matches, but near-duplicates are the vendor's
              responsibility.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="lifecycle" class="mt-10">
        <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-ink-900 mb-3">Lifecycle &amp; permissions</h2>
        <p>
          The registry distinguishes between <strong>additive</strong> operations, which vendors
          can perform, and <strong>structural</strong> operations, which are admin-only.
        </p>

        <h3 class="mt-6 mb-2 text-base font-semibold text-ink-900">What vendors can do</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Request a new attribute</strong> — Submit a request for a
            registry-level attribute that doesn't exist yet. The request enters a review queue
            and is visible to the platform admin team.</li>
          <li><strong>Add a value</strong> to any existing active attribute — Immediately
            usable, immediately available to every vendor.</li>
          <li><strong>Edit a value you created</strong> — Correct typos, fix metadata, or
            adjust the value's details.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3 class="mt-6 mb-2 text-base font-semibold text-ink-900">What only admins can do</h3>

        <div class="callout callout-danger">
          <i data-lucide="shield-alert" class="w-5 h-5 callout-icon"></i>
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed">
              <strong>Once an attribute or value is in the registry, only privileged admin
                users can remove, disable, or update it.</strong> This applies to system-defined
              attributes and values, and to vendor-added values once they've been adopted
              across listings.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <p>Specifically, admin-only operations include:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Deleting an attribute</strong> — Removes it from the registry entirely.</li>
          <li><strong>Deleting a value</strong> — Removes a specific option from an attribute.</li>
          <li><strong>Disabling an attribute or value</strong> — Sets it to
            <code>inactive</code> so it no longer appears for new listings, while preserving
            existing products that reference it.
          </li>
          <li><strong>Renaming an attribute or value</strong> — Changing the canonical title
            or value text.</li>
          <li><strong>Editing attribute metadata</strong> — Description, display order, or
            verification state.</li>
          <li><strong>Approving or denying requests</strong> — Reviewing vendor-submitted
            attribute requests.</li>
        </ul>

        <p>
          The reason for this split is stability. If any vendor could rename or delete a
          registry attribute, every other vendor's listings would break silently. The audit
          trail on every attribute and value ensures changes are traceable to a specific
          privileged user.
        </p>

        <div class="callout callout-info">
          <i data-lucide="history" class="w-5 h-5 callout-icon"></i>
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed">
              <strong>Nothing is hard-deleted by default.</strong> When admin staff disable an
              attribute or value, existing listings continue to render correctly — the option
              simply stops appearing in the picker for new listings. Hard deletes are reserved
              for duplicates and errors caught early.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="metadata" class="mt-10">
        <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-ink-900 mb-3">Value metadata</h2>
        <p>
          Each value can carry optional <strong>metadata</strong> — a small set of key-value
          pairs that add structure without requiring a new attribute. Metadata is per-value,
          flexible, and only as strict as you make it.
        </p>
        <p>Common uses:</p>

        <div class="rounded-xl border border-ink-200 divide-y divide-ink-100 overflow-hidden not-prose">
          <div class="px-4 py-3">
            <p class="text-xs font-semibold text-ink-700">Color codes</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-ink-500 mt-0.5">
              Value <em>Red</em> → <code>{ "code": "#FF0000" }</code> — enables color swatches
              in filters and product pages.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="px-4 py-3">
            <p class="text-xs font-semibold text-ink-700">Pros and cons</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-ink-500 mt-0.5">
              Value <em>DDR4</em> → <code>{ "pros": "Cheaper, widely supported", "cons": "Slower than DDR5" }</code>
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="px-4 py-3">
            <p class="text-xs font-semibold text-ink-700">Numeric equivalents</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-ink-500 mt-0.5">
              Value <em>15.6"</em> → <code>{ "inches": "15.6" }</code> — helps with range filters.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="px-4 py-3">
            <p class="text-xs font-semibold text-ink-700">External references</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-ink-500 mt-0.5">
              Value <em>iPhone 15</em> → <code>{ "ref": "apple.com/iphone-15" }</code>
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <h3 class="mt-6 mb-2 text-base font-semibold text-ink-900">Metadata rules</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Optional</strong> — A value with no metadata is perfectly valid.</li>
          <li><strong>Key-value pairs</strong> — Keys and values are both strings.</li>
          <li><strong>Keys must be unique</strong> within a single value's metadata.</li>
          <li><strong>Keep keys short and predictable</strong> — <code>code</code>,
            <code>capacity</code>, <code>hex</code> — not sentences.
          </li>
          <li><strong>No nested structures</strong> — Flat objects only. If you need hierarchy,
            that's a signal you need a new attribute, not deeper metadata.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section id="dos" class="mt-10">
        <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-ink-900 mb-3">Do &amp; don't</h2>

        <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4 not-prose">
          <div class="rounded-xl border border-emerald-200 bg-emerald-50/40 p-4">
            <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-3">
              <i data-lucide="check-circle-2" class="w-4 h-4 text-emerald-600"></i>
              <p class="text-xs font-semibold text-emerald-700 uppercase tracking-wider">Do</p>
            </div>
            <ul class="space-y-2 text-sm text-emerald-900">
              <li>Search the registry before requesting an attribute.</li>
              <li>Match the naming conventions of sibling values.</li>
              <li>Write descriptions that explain when to use the attribute.</li>
              <li>Add metadata when it improves filtering or display.</li>
              <li>Fix your own values if you spot a typo.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>

          <div class="rounded-xl border border-red-200 bg-red-50/40 p-4">
            <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-3">
              <i data-lucide="x-circle" class="w-4 h-4 text-red-600"></i>
              <p class="text-xs font-semibold text-red-700 uppercase tracking-wider">Don't</p>
            </div>
            <ul class="space-y-2 text-sm text-red-900">
              <li>Request attributes that are really product names.</li>
              <li>Add duplicate values with different casing or spacing.</li>
              <li>Use attributes for marketing copy or subjective claims.</li>
              <li>Rename or delete a value once listings depend on it.</li>
              <li>Pack multiple meanings into a single value or key.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="faq" class="mt-10">
        <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-ink-900 mb-3">FAQ</h2>

        <div class="space-y-5">
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-ink-900">What's the difference between an attribute and a category?</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-ink-500 mt-1">
              Categories group products (<em>Phones</em>, <em>Laptops</em>). Attributes describe
              them (<em>Color</em>, <em>RAM Type</em>). A product lives in one category but has
              many attributes.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-ink-900">Can I delete a value I just added?</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-ink-500 mt-1">
              If you created it and no listings depend on it yet, yes. Once a value is used in
              live listings, deletion is admin-only to protect those listings from breaking.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-ink-900">What happens to my listings if an attribute is disabled?</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-ink-500 mt-1">
              Nothing. Your listings keep rendering with the value they already have. The
              attribute simply stops appearing in the picker when new listings are created.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-ink-900">How long does a request take to be reviewed?</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-ink-500 mt-1">
              The admin team aims to review requests within a few business days. You'll be
              notified when your request is approved or denied, with a reason if denied.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-ink-900">Can two attributes have the same value?</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-ink-500 mt-1">
              Yes. <em>Silver</em> can exist as a value under <code>Color</code> and also under
              <code>Material</code>. Uniqueness is enforced per-attribute, not globally.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-ink-900">Why is my new attribute marked "requested" instead of "active"?</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-ink-500 mt-1">
              All new attributes enter the registry as requests. Only admin staff can promote a
              request to <code>active</code>, which is when it becomes available to vendors
              for listing products.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

